ransport","units":"Per cent","pts":[[1767225600,0.09],[1769904000,-0.05],[1772323200,0],[1775001600,-0.01],[1777593600,1.75],[1780272000,4.32],[1782864000,4.44],[1785542400,4.61],[1788220800,5.01]]},{"id":"divinf_7","name":"Information and communication","units":"Per cent","pts":[[1767225600,0.16],[1769904000,0.25],[1772323200,0.33],[1775001600,0.5],[1777593600,0.3],[1780272000,0.43],[1782864000,0.63],[1785542400,2],[1788220800,2.44]]},{"id":"divinf_8","name":"Recreation, sport and culture","units":"Per cent","pts":[[1767225600,2.32],[1769904000,2.21],[1772323200,2.47],[1775001600,2.11],[1777593600,1.98],[1780272000,1.73],[1782864000,1.64],[1785542400,1.73],[1788220800,2.16]]},{"id":"divinf_9","name":"Education services","units":"Per cent","pts":[[1767225600,3.35],[1769904000,3.33],[1772323200,3.31],[1775001600,3.15],[1777593600,2.99],[1780272000,3.35],[1782864000,3.66],[1785542400,3.74],[1788220800,3.94]]},{"id":"divinf_10","name":"Restaurants & accommodation","units":"Per cent","pts":[[1767225600,2.87],[1769904000,2.73],[1772323200,2.88],[1775001600,4.22],[1777593600,5.74],[1780272000,6.91],[1782864000,7.72],[1785542400,8.38],[1788220800,9.36]]},{"id":"divinf_11","name":"Personal care & misc. goods/services","units":"Per cent","pts":[[1767225600,19.06],[1769904000,19.65],[1772323200,18.63],[1775001600,17.66],[1777593600,18.46],[1780272000,16.72],[1782864000,14.78],[1785542400,15.15],[1788220800,13.75]]}]}],"qna":[{"question":"What is this series and why does it start in 2025?","answer":"The Consumer Price Index measures the change in retail prices of a fixed basket of goods and services bought by households. MoSPI re-based it from 2012 to 2024=100 in February 2026, updating the basket (358 items, including airfares, OTT subscriptions and online purchases) and weights from the 2023-24 household consumption survey.^Index values are published from January 2025 because 2024 is the base year; MoSPI also publishes a separate 'back series' back to 2013 (see the linked back-series page), which is a conversion of the old basket and not a like-for-like history."},{"question":"How should I read it for nowcasting?","answer":"Use the Combined general index for the headline, and the rural/urban split to see where price pressure sits. Official inflation is the percentage change from the same month a year earlier; because the new index only begins in January 2025 it is available from January 2026 only.^The divisions let you track pass-through: food and beverages dominates month-to-month swings, while housing, transport, health and education move more slowly. Weights differ by sector (food carries a larger weight in rural India)."},{"question":"What are the main caveats?","answer":"Groups are not comparable with the 2012-base series (for example there is no 'fuel and light' group; electricity, gas and other fuels sit within division 4 housing, and there is no 'miscellaneous' group). Do not splice this series onto the old one; ask MoSPI's linking factor if a continuous series is required.^Recent months can be revised (the figure for the latest month is provisional), and the series is short, so seasonal patterns cannot yet be estimated reliably from it alone."}],"related":[],"layer":"added","scraped_at":"2026-10-30T07:09:32+00:00"}